1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,440
Announcing Aomi Task API.

2
00:00:06,490 --> 00:00:08,168
Here's a treasury agent on Arc

3
00:00:08,208 --> 00:00:09,945
sending one long instruction:

4
00:00:09,985 --> 00:00:14,159
rebalance into EURC, JPYC and MXNB,

5
00:00:14,199 --> 00:00:19,697
fill within 15 bps of mid, keep 25,000 USDC for payroll,

6
00:00:19,737 --> 00:00:22,769
and revert everything if any leg fails.

7
00:00:25,019 --> 00:00:28,114
Aomi's harness turns that into transactions

8
00:00:28,154 --> 00:00:30,203
across StableFX and Arc pools,

9
00:00:30,243 --> 00:00:32,491
so the agent never writes calldata.

10
00:00:32,531 --> 00:00:35,834
Then it runs the whole batch on an internal fork of Arc,

11
00:00:35,874 --> 00:00:39,255
and checks every constraint against the result.

12
00:00:40,505 --> 00:00:42,566
What comes back is an outcome fingerprint,

13
00:00:42,606 --> 00:00:48,964
signed by Aomi: 22,000 USDC out, EURC and JPYC in,

14
00:00:49,004 --> 00:00:52,853
every constraint passed, and the MXNB leg skipped,

15
00:00:52,893 --> 00:00:56,301
because the best fill was 19 bps from mid.

16
00:00:56,341 --> 00:00:58,576
Getting this far costs one cent.

17
00:00:58,616 --> 00:01:00,155
The buyer sees the outcome before

18
00:01:00,195 --> 00:01:02,170
anything touches its wallet.

19
00:01:04,021 --> 00:01:05,200
Now the buyer decides.

20
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:06,233
If it wants the batch,

21
00:01:06,273 --> 00:01:09,600
it pays 3% of the USDC leaving its wallet,

22
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:15,842
here 660 USDC, over x402, and gets the full bundle.

23
00:01:18,692 --> 00:01:21,891
Then it signs and broadcasts the bundle itself.

24
00:01:21,931 --> 00:01:24,840
Aomi never holds its keys or its funds.

25
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:27,023
What Aomi sells is the work:

26
00:01:27,063 --> 00:01:30,842
a harness that runs complex money-lego intents faster,

27
00:01:30,882 --> 00:01:33,949
more reliably, and with fewer tokens.

28
00:01:34,799 --> 00:01:37,011
Watch a real run on Arc Mainnet.

29
00:01:37,051 --> 00:01:38,764
The agent asks to send 0.1 and

30
00:01:38,804 --> 00:01:41,702
0.2 USDC to two ops wallets,

31
00:01:41,742 --> 00:01:45,696
and pays one cent over x402 to prepare.

32
00:01:45,736 --> 00:01:49,191
It polls for free until the simulation passes every check.

33
00:01:49,231 --> 00:01:52,348
Retrieval costs 3% of the thirty cents moved,

34
00:01:52,388 --> 00:01:54,961
so 0.009 USDC.

35
00:01:55,001 --> 00:01:58,618
Then its own wallet signs, both transfers confirm onchain,

36
00:01:58,658 --> 00:02:01,202
and the recipient balances are verified.

37
00:02:02,052 --> 00:02:03,382
Why does this matter?

38
00:02:03,422 --> 00:02:06,656
x402 already lets agents pay for data.

39
00:02:06,696 --> 00:02:09,280
The chain sits in between as a neutral rail,

40
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:11,876
so neither side has to trust the other.

41
00:02:12,426 --> 00:02:14,743
But what if an agent wants to buy a service?

42
00:02:14,783 --> 00:02:16,438
The outcome of the work cannot

43
00:02:16,478 --> 00:02:19,185
be pre-verified before delivery.

44
00:02:22,635 --> 00:02:24,162
People haven't solved this either.

45
00:02:24,202 --> 00:02:25,904
You cannot tell whether a contractor

46
00:02:25,944 --> 00:02:28,558
does good jobs before seeing them work.

47
00:02:31,208 --> 00:02:34,036
This loop never completes in the human economy.

48
00:02:34,076 --> 00:02:35,162
Machines can do better,

49
00:02:35,202 --> 00:02:36,915
because their service is usually

50
00:02:36,955 --> 00:02:39,040
the result of processing data,

51
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:41,327
which can be checked before it's delivered.

52
00:02:41,367 --> 00:02:44,097
That's the loop the Task API runs.

53
00:02:44,947 --> 00:02:46,718
A transparent settlement layer,

54
00:02:46,758 --> 00:02:50,004
plus machine work that can be checked before delivery,

55
00:02:50,044 --> 00:02:53,749
makes outcome-based pricing for services possible.

56
00:02:55,999 --> 00:02:58,525
One trust assumption remains: today,

57
00:02:58,565 --> 00:03:02,449
the buyer trusts that Aomi's simulation ran honestly.

58
00:03:02,489 --> 00:03:04,574
Next, we plan to run the harness in

59
00:03:04,614 --> 00:03:06,791
a trusted execution environment,

60
00:03:06,831 --> 00:03:09,724
so buying a service becomes trustless.

61
00:03:11,774 --> 00:03:15,403
The Task API is live, settling in USDC on Arc.

62
00:03:15,443 --> 00:03:17,929
Try it at aomi.dev.
